{
  "id": "ar_practical_eg",
  "title": "Arabic — practical Egyptian romanization",
  "language": "ar",
  "script": "Arab",
  "kind": "practical",
  "status": "informal",
  "ascii": true,
  "authority": "Egyptian document and press practice; Survey of Egypt System (SES) conventions as summarised by UNGEGN; not a formal standard",
  "description": "Egyptian-style ASCII spellings: g for ج (Gamal), article el- (Sharm el-Sheikh), ay = ei (Zeinab, Hussein), ū = ou (Mahmoud), ة = a, Abd el-X = Abdel X (Abdel Rahman, Gamal Abdel Nasser). Known names come from the lexicon (latin['ar_eg'], then latin['ar']).",
  "sources": [
    "https://www.eki.ee/wgrs/rom1_ar.pdf", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romanization_of_Arabic",
    "https://thenationalnews.com/uae/other-countries-grapple-with-same-issue-1.331487"
  ],
  "notes": [
    "SES values used: j = g, ay = ei, -ah = -a, article always el- (no assimilation). ū is written ou (Mahmoud, Abdel Ghafour) as in common Egyptian spellings; SES itself has û.",
    "Kasra before a word-final consonant is written e (Khaled, Adel, Hatem); intervocalic s/ṣ is written ss (Hassan, Nasser, Essam) except after an initial vowel (Osama). These follow common Egyptian/French-influenced usage rather than SES.",
    "Other vowel colouring (Ahmed, Mohamed, Mostafa) is lexical and is left to the name lexicon.",
    "ق stays q (SES primary value); the frequent Egyptian k (Tarek, Farouk) is not applied."
  ],
  "alphabet": "ءآأؤإئابةتثجحخدذرزسشصضطظعغفقكلمنهوىيٱپچژگڤ",
  "hook": "arabic",
